WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:06.150
Vsauce! Kevin here, with a serial killer who
may very well have murdered hundreds. Her

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:12.910
systematic killing spree went undetected for
years because suspicion just wasn’t enough.

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:27.250
What finally brought her to justice was…
math.

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:33.371
In the United States, the most dangerous place
you can go is… the hospital. Yes, doctors

00:00:33.371 --> 00:00:39.710
and nurses save lives literally every single
day, but medical error is a significant cause

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:46.559
of death. No matter how hard we try, tragedies
happen -- and by definition, everyone in a

00:00:46.559 --> 00:00:54.020
hospital is already sick with something. But
murder isn’t an accident, and a nurse in

00:00:54.020 --> 00:01:00.379
Massachusetts was straight-up killing patients…
to impress a guy.

00:01:00.379 --> 00:01:04.850
Kristen Gilbert was a nurse at the Veterans
Administration Medical Center in Northampton,

00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:10.040
Massachusetts. She was popular with co-workers.
She’d go out clubbing with them in Boston

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:16.940
to blow off steam, and even threw baby showers.
She also seemed to be really good at her job

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:23.960
-- it felt like she was always around to save
the day in an emergency. She appeared to be

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:25.909
the perfect nurse.

00:01:25.909 --> 00:01:32.640
But Gilbert was around so often when patients
died that other nurses jokingly referred to

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.920
her as the “Angel of Death.”

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:43.810
It seemed like the Northampton hospital had
a lot of unexpected deaths… but a VA investigation

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:50.049
found that the hospital’s rate of deaths
was in line with similar VA hospitals.

00:01:50.049 --> 00:01:53.979
So. Case closed, right? WRONG.

00:01:53.979 --> 00:02:00.420
Doctors and nurses weren’t convinced, but
there were no witnesses who saw Gilbert actually

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:08.899
do anything suspicious to patients. There
wasn’t any evidence. But there was data.

00:02:08.899 --> 00:02:14.510
Here’s a question: how often did patients
die when Gilbert was on the floor, compared

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:21.470
to when she wasn’t? Was there a correlation
between her presence and patient deaths? And

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:28.849
if so… was it just an unlucky coincidence?
Or was she killing people?

00:02:28.849 --> 00:02:33.950
The first analysis can be expressed visually
-- this graph shows patient deaths in the

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:43.290
hospital ward between 1988 and 1997 broken
down into 3 shifts: day, evening, and overnight.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:50.139
The 4pm - midnight evening shift stands out
as having the most deaths, and Kristen Gilbert

00:02:50.139 --> 00:02:52.400
worked the evening shift.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:58.209
But that doesn’t hold for every year. 1988
and 1989 don’t match up with the 6 years

00:02:58.209 --> 00:03:03.530
that follow it. There weren’t that many
deaths, and they were pretty evenly distributed

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:05.040
throughout the day.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:09.169
Kristen Gilbert didn’t start working on
the ward until 1990.

00:03:09.169 --> 00:03:15.889
Okay. Okay. What about the really high number
of deaths during overnight shifts in 1990?

00:03:15.889 --> 00:03:22.860
That’s an outlier in this whole data set
-- Gilbert worked evenings, but when she started

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:28.680
in 1990, she worked the overnight shift.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:34.829
HUH. HUH. HUH.

00:03:34.829 --> 00:03:40.900
But this graph is still not proof. Variation
in human outcomes is tremendous, whether it’s

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:47.590
education or physical fitness or death. It’s
totally possible that Kristen Gilbert was

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:53.040
present during a statistical blip. It’s
possible there’s some nurse out there working

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:59.619
on a floor where patients never seem to die,
like some kind of Angel of Life! Just ask

00:03:59.619 --> 00:04:03.840
Brandon Mayfield whether coincidences happen.
Or the FBI.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:09.609
Dr. Stephen Gehlbach, who is now Dean Emeritus
of UMass-Amherst’s School of Public Health,

00:04:09.609 --> 00:04:16.359
ran the numbers. He looked at 1,641 nursing
shifts over the period of 18 months leading

00:04:16.359 --> 00:04:23.060
up to staff taking their concerns to hospital
management. There were 74 deaths during those

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:31.470
shifts -- which is a death on 4.51% of all
shifts, or .045 deaths per shift.

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:38.860
Kristen Gilbert worked 257 shifts during that
18 month period. With .045 deaths per shift,

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:46.539
we’d expect her to be present for about
11 or 12 deaths -- about 1 every 6 weeks on

00:04:46.539 --> 00:04:47.539
average.

00:04:47.539 --> 00:04:54.630
15 or 16 deaths when you expect 11 or 12 could
be an unlucky statistical anomaly. She was

00:04:54.630 --> 00:05:04.539
there for 40. FORTY! A patient died on over
15% of Gilbert’s shifts. What are the chances

00:05:04.539 --> 00:05:07.939
of THAT? We’ll find out in a second.

